# Spring Boot with MongoDB: Step-by-Step

## Description
This project demonstrates how to integrate MongoDB with a Spring Boot application, focusing on CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations on a `Product` entity.

## Table of Contents
- Installation
- Usage
- Endpoints
- Models
- Services
- Video Demonstration
- Contributing
- Contact Information

## Installation

### Prerequisites
- Java Development Kit (JDK) 17 or later
- Maven or Gradle
- MongoDB installed on your machine (or running via Docker)
- Spring Boot (version 3.2+ recommended)
- IDE (IntelliJ IDEA, Eclipse, etc.)

### Steps
1. **Clone the repository**:
   ```bash
   git clone https://github.com/yourusername/your-repo-name.git
   cd your-repo-name
Install dependencies:

mvn install
Start MongoDB:

Using Docker:
docker run --name mongodb -d -p 27017:27017 mongo
Or install MongoDB locally following the official instructions.
Run the application:

mvn spring-boot:run
Usage
To interact with the API, you can use tools like Postman or cURL.

Endpoints
GET /products/getAllProducts: Retrieves all products.
GET /products/{id}: Retrieves a product by ID.
PUT /products/updateProduct/{id}: Updates a product by ID.
DELETE /products/deleteProduct/{id}: Deletes a product by ID.
POST /products/addProduct: Adds a new product.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Models
Product
package com.example.mongodb.model;

import org.springframework.data.annotation.Id;
import org.springframework.data.mongodb.core.mapping.Document;

@Document(collection = "products")
public class Product {
    @Id
    private String id;
    private String name;
    private String description;
    private double price;

    // Getters and setters
}
Services
ProductService
Handles the business logic for managing products.
